✅ 28. Print Numbers Using Three Threads — Remainder Problem

This question extends the Odd–Even Problem from 2 threads to 3 threads.
The core synchronization pattern remains the same: shared state → condition check → wait() → perform work → update state → notifyAll().
This is the same synchronization template used in the Producer–Consumer problem.

🎗️ 28.1 Problem Statement

Implement a program to print numbers sequentially using three threads.
Each thread is responsible for numbers based on the remainder obtained by dividing the current number by 3.

Responsibilities
------------------------------------------------------
| Thread        | Condition         | Numbers        |
| ------------- | ----------------- | -------------- |
| `Remainder-1` | `number % 3 == 1` | 1, 4, 7, 10... |
| `Remainder-2` | `number % 3 == 2` | 2, 5, 8, 11... |
| `Remainder-0` | `number % 3 == 0` | 3, 6, 9, 12... |
------------------------------------------------------

Expected Output

For N = 12:

Remainder-1 → 1
Remainder-2 → 2
Remainder-0 → 3
Remainder-1 → 4
Remainder-2 → 5
Remainder-0 → 6
Remainder-1 → 7
Remainder-2 → 8
Remainder-0 → 9
Remainder-1 → 10
Remainder-2 → 11
Remainder-0 → 12

The important point is:

Three different threads are responsible for printing, but the numbers must remain sequential.

🎗️ 28.2 Why Is This Question Asked?

👉 This is basically the generalized version of the Odd–Even problem.

Odd–Even Problem
2 Threads
    ↓
number % 2
    ↓
Odd / Even

👉 Remainder Problem

3 Threads
    ↓
number % 3
    ↓
0 / 1 / 2

So the interviewer is checking whether you understand how to generalize thread coordination instead of hardcoding logic for only two threads.

