🎗️ 28.6 Ownership Rule

This is the heart of the problem.
Each thread owns one remainder.

-----------------------------------------------------
| Thread        | Remainder | Condition             |
| ------------- | --------: | --------------------- |
| `Remainder-0` |       `0` | `currNumber % 3 == 0` |
| `Remainder-1` |       `1` | `currNumber % 3 == 1` |
| `Remainder-2` |       `2` | `currNumber % 3 == 2` |
----------------------------------------------------

👉 Example 1

currNumber = 7
7 % 3 = 1

Therefore:
Remainder-1 prints 7

👉 Example 2

currNumber = 8
8 % 3 = 2

Therefore:
Remainder-2 prints 8

👉 Example 3

currNumber = 9
9 % 3 = 0

Therefore:
Remainder-0 prints 9

🎗️ 28.7 Why Do We Need synchronized?

🔴 Your method is: printRemainder()
🔴 and it is declared: synchronized
🔴 That means only one thread at a time can execute the synchronized method on the same RemainderManager object.

Conceptually:

  RemainderManager
        |
      Monitor
   /     |     \
 T0      T1      T2

🔴 Only one thread can hold the monitor at a time.
🔴 This prevents multiple threads from simultaneously modifying: currNumber
🔴 Without synchronization, two threads could potentially read/update the shared counter concurrently, creating a race condition.

🎗️ 28.8 Why wait()?

🔴 Suppose: currNumber = 5
🔴 Now: 5 % 3 = 2

👉 Therefore:

🔴 Remainder-2 → My turn
🔴 Remainder-0 → Not my turn
🔴 Remainder-1 → Not my turn

👉 The threads whose remainder does not match must wait.

Thread
   ↓
Check remainder
   ↓
Is it my turn?
   ↓
   NO
   ↓
 wait()

wait() causes the thread to enter the WAITING state and releases the monitor so another thread can acquire it.

🎗️ 28.9 Why while Instead of if?

👉 Your code correctly uses:

while(currNumber % 3 != number && currNumber <= limit)

👉 instead of:

if(currNumber % 3 != number) {
    wait();
}

🔴 This is important. The thread must re-check the condition after waking up.

wait()
   ↓
Wake Up
   ↓
Re-check remainder
   ↓
Is it my turn?
   ↓
YES → Continue
NO  → wait() again

👉 Reasons include:

🔴 Spurious wakeups
🔴 Multiple threads being awakened
🔴 Another thread changing the shared state before the awakened thread reacquires the monitor

🎗️ 28.10 Why notifyAll()?

🔴 After a thread prints a number: currNumber++;
🔴 the ownership of the next number changes.

👉 For example:

currNumber = 5
Thread-2 prints: 5

Then: currNumber = 6
Now: 6 % 3 = 0

So Thread-0 should get the opportunity to print.

👉  Therefore:

Print
 ↓
currNumber++
 ↓
notifyAll()
 ↓
Waiting threads wake
 ↓
Each checks its condition
 ↓
Correct thread proceeds

This is why your code uses: notifyAll();
rather than simply continuing.

🎗️ 28.11 Why notifyAll() Instead of notify()?

👉 Suppose:

Remainder-0
Remainder-1
Remainder-2

are waiting.

🔴 After one thread increments currNumber, we don't know which waiting thread should proceed.

👉 For example:

currNumber = 7
7 % 3 = 1

Only:
🔴 Remainder-1 should print.
🔴 If notify() wakes the wrong thread:

Remainder-0 wakes
  ↓
Checks 7 % 3 == 0
  ↓
false
  ↓
waits again

🔴 The correct thread may still remain waiting.
🔴 With: notifyAll() all waiting threads get an opportunity to re-check their conditions.

notifyAll()
      ↓
All waiting threads wake
      ↓
Check condition
      ↓
Correct remainder thread proceeds

This is exactly the reason notifyAll() is preferred when multiple waiting thread types may exist.

🎗️ 28.12 Complete Synchronization Logic

👉 The complete logic of your printRemainder() method is:

Thread enters
     ↓
Acquire monitor
     ↓
currNumber <= limit?
     ↓
Check my remainder
     ↓
Not my turn?
     ↓
wait()
     ↓
Wake up
     ↓
Re-check condition
     ↓
My turn?
     ↓
Print number
     ↓
currNumber++
     ↓
notifyAll()
     ↓
return true
