Thread calls method again

🎗️ 28.13 Understanding printRemainder(int number)

The parameter: number represents the remainder owned by the thread.

👉 For example:

printRemainder(0)
means: I am responsible for remainder 0

👉 Similarly:

printRemainder(1)
means: I am responsible for remainder 1

printRemainder(2)
means: I am responsible for remainder 2

So instead of creating three different methods, you have one generic method.
That's a very good design choice.

🎗️ 28.14 Thread Creation

Your main method creates three threads:

Thread-0 → printRemainder(0)
Thread-1 → printRemainder(1)
Thread-2 → printRemainder(2)

👉 Conceptually:

RemainderManager
       |-- currNumber
       |-- limit
       ├── Remainder-0 → remainder 0
       ├── Remainder-1 → remainder 1
       └── Remainder-2 → remainder 2

All three threads share the same manager object.

🎗️ 28.15 Why while(printRemainder(...)) ?

🔴 Your thread code uses: while(remainderManager.printRemainder(0));

This means:

Call printRemainder()
 ↓
Returns true?
 ↓
Yes
 ↓
Call again
 ↓
...
 ↓
Returns false
 ↓
Thread terminates

🔴 Your printRemainder() returns: true after successfully printing a number.
🔴 When: currNumber > limit it eventually returns: false
🔴 which terminates the thread's loop.

So the return value acts as a continuation signal.

🎗️ 28.16 Why currNumber++?

🔴 After printing: currNumber the shared number must move to the next number.

For example:

5
 ↓
print 5
 ↓
currNumber++
 ↓
6

🔴 Then: 6 % 3 = 0 So the remainder-0 thread gets its turn.
🔴 Without incrementing: currNumber the same number would remain forever and the program could never progress.

