🎗️ 28.23 Common Mistakes

❌ Creating three separate counters
❌ Creating three different RemainderManager objects
❌ Using if instead of while
❌ Forgetting notifyAll()
❌ Forgetting currNumber++
❌ Calling wait() outside synchronized context
❌ Using different monitor objects
❌ Hardcoding separate methods for remainder 0, 1 and 2
❌ Using busy waiting
❌ Calling run() instead of start()

🎗️ 28.24 Why One Shared Counter?

This is extremely important.

👉 Suppose we used:

Thread-0 → counter0
Thread-1 → counter1
Thread-2 → counter2

Then there would be no single source of truth for the sequence.

Instead:

 currNumber
  /   |   \
 /    |    \
T0     T1     T2

All threads look at the same current number.
Therefore, they can determine:

Whose turn is it?
based on: currNumber % 3

🎗️ 28.25 Why One Common Monitor?

All threads must coordinate through the same shared state.

Therefore:
RemainderManager
      ↓
    Monitor
   /   |   \
 T0    T1    T2

If each thread synchronized on a different object, they would not properly coordinate with each other.

🎗️ 28.26 Can We Generalize This?

Yes. This is the most important extension.

👉 Instead of:
3 Threads

👉 we can have:
N Threads
Each thread can own a remainder.

👉 Conceptually:

number % N == threadId

For example, with:

N = 5

we can have:

Thread-0 → remainder 0
Thread-1 → remainder 1
Thread-2 → remainder 2
Thread-3 → remainder 3
Thread-4 → remainder 4

This is the foundation for the more generalized cyclic execution of N threads problem.

🎗️ 28.27 Advantages

✔ Demonstrates inter-thread communication
✔ Demonstrates condition-based synchronization
✔ Prevents race conditions on the shared counter
✔ Maintains sequential output
✔ Uses one shared resource
✔ Easy to generalize
✔ Demonstrates wait() and notifyAll()
✔ Extends the Odd–Even synchronization pattern

🎗️ 28.28 Disadvantages

❌ Multiple threads may spend time waiting
❌ notifyAll() can wake threads that cannot proceed
❌ More threads can cause additional context switching
❌ Incorrect synchronization can cause deadlocks or progress issues
❌ Logic becomes more complex as the number of threads increases

🎗️ 28.29 Best Practices

✔ Use one shared manager object
✔ Use one shared counter
✔ Assign each thread a remainder
✔ Keep the synchronization logic inside RemainderManager
✔ Use synchronized
✔ Always use while before wait()
✔ Use notifyAll() after changing shared state
✔ Do not use busy waiting
✔ Parameterize the remainder instead of creating duplicate methods
✔ Make the starting number and limit configurable

